The Nautilus system administrators team has rolled out a web UI application for their backup utility on the Nautilus application server 2 within the Stratos Datacenter. 
This application runs on port 8084and appropriate firewall rules must be configured to allow incoming traffic. 
To achieve this, firewalld needs to be installed and configured on the application server. 
To ensure proper functionality, the following requirements have been identified:

Install and enable the firewalld service.
Allow all incoming connections on port 8084/tcp.
Ensure the zone is set to public.

SOLUTION
To configure the firewall on Nautilus App Server 2, run the following commands on the server.

🛠️ Configuration Steps
Install firewalld

bash
sudo yum install firewalld -y
Start and enable the firewalld service

bash
sudo systemctl enable --now firewalld
Set the default zone to public

bash
sudo firewall-cmd --set-default-zone=public
Allow incoming connections on port 8084/tcp

bash
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --zone=public --add-port=8084/tcp
Reload firewalld to apply the changes

bash
sudo firewall-cmd --reload
🔍 Verification
After completing the steps, you can verify the configuration with these commands:

bash
# Check the default zone
sudo firewall-cmd --get-default-zone

# Check that the port is open in the public zone
sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --query-port=8084/tcp

# List all rules for the public zone
sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --list-all
The last command should show 8084/tcp in the ports: line.
